  data:  # 9deb
    kind: invariant  # 9deb
    title: "Rust module paths are emitted in two normalizations in one run (imports use '::', module_attr_ref uses '.'), so the catalogued stdlib reads as uncatalogued"  # 9deb
    status: violated  # 9deb
    priority: 1  # 9deb
    parent:  # 9deb
    tags:  # 9deb
    - io_boundary  # 9deb
    - verify_claims  # 9deb
    - rust  # 9deb
    - identifier_format  # 9deb
    isbefore: []  # 9deb
    duplicate_of: []  # 9deb
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 9deb
    pr_ref:  # 9deb
    description: "MEASURED 2026-08-23, same fixture and run as the sibling item:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/rust_fidelity_probe_08232026/.\n\nTWO NORMALIZATIONS OF ONE FACT, VISIBLE IN A SINGLE RUN'S EDGE SET:\n\n  imports          rust:std::net::UdpSocket:0-0:module:external_symbol\n  module_attr_ref  rust:std.net:0-0:std.net.UdpSocket:external_symbol\n\nrust.yaml declares modules in the `::` spelling (std::net::UdpSocket,\nstd::io::Write, std::fs). The colon form MATCHES -- std::fs is absent from the\ngate's uncatalogued list and the fs_read control fires correctly (violated, 1\nchain). The dotted form matches nothing, and std / std.io / std.net are\nreported as modules \"the I/O catalog could not classify\".\n\nSo the Rust STANDARD LIBRARY reads as an uncatalogued third-party dependency,\non a crate that has no dependencies.\n\nRELATION TO THE SIBLING. That item is about module_attr_ref being counted at\nall; this one is about the id it carries. They are independent: fixing the\nedge-type set would stop these particular rows reaching the gate, and would\nleave the dual normalization in place for any other consumer that reads a\nmodule_attr_ref dst. Fixing the normalization would leave imports being counted\nas calls. Both are needed, and neither is a workaround for the other.\n\nWHERE TO LOOK, not yet read in code: whichever producer mints module_attr_ref\ndsts is applying a dotted (python-shaped) module convention to a language whose\nseparator is `::`. LIVE.md already records \"Rust '::' by-design\" in the\nINV-dulah id-grammar context, so the colon form is the intended one.\n\nNOT MEASURED: whether any language other than rust carries a separator the\ndotted convention corrupts; whether consumers other than the coverage gate read\nmodule_attr_ref dsts and are equally affected; whether is_stdlib_module has an\nindependent rust gap underneath this one."  # 9deb
    fields:  # 9deb
      statement: "A module name must have ONE spelling across every edge in a run, and it must be the spelling the language's I/O catalog declares."  # 9deb
      root_cause: "module_attr_ref dsts apply a dotted module convention to Rust, whose separator is '::' and whose io_primitives/rust.yaml rows are declared with '::'. imports edges in the same run use '::' and match correctly, so the two spellings coexist and the dotted one silently fails every catalogue lookup."  # 9deb
- op: discuss  # bddd
  at: '2026-08-24T03:24:25Z'  # bddd
  by: agent  # bddd
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bddd
  clock: 2  # bddd
  nonce: bddd  # bddd
  message: "STATED CONSEQUENCE REFUTED; THE OBSERVATION SURVIVES. Measured directly\nagainst the shipped catalogue rather than inferred from the uncatalogued list:\n\n  dst passed to classify_call                                verdict\n  rust:std.fs:0-0:read_to_string:external_symbol             MATCH fs_read\n  rust:std::fs:0-0:read_to_string:external_symbol            MATCH fs_read\n  rust:std.net.UdpSocket:0-0:send:external_symbol            MATCH net_send\n  rust:std::net::UdpSocket:0-0:send:external_symbol          MATCH net_send\n  rust:std.env:0-0:std.env.consts:attribute                  MATCH env_read\n\nTHE DOTTED FORM MATCHES. IoBoundaryCatalog registers both `::` and `.` in its\nqualified-name index exactly as emit_module_attribute_refs' docstring claims,\nand this item's root_cause (\"the dotted one silently fails every catalogue\nlookup\") is false. So is the headline consequence: the Rust standard library\ndoes not read as uncatalogued BECAUSE of the spelling.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY PUT std / std.io / std.net ON THE LIST, both filed on INV-pusin:\nproducer artifacts (`use` paths and return-type paths emitted as attribute\nreads), and rust.yaml declaring ZERO module_completeness entries -- so\nmodule_io_is_enumerated returns False for EVERY rust module in BOTH spellings\n(std, std::io, std.io, std::net, std.net, std::fs, std.fs, std::env, std.env\nall False). Neither is a normalization problem.\n\nTwo of the three non-matches are CORRECT rather than defects:\n  rust:std.net:0-0:std.net.UdpSocket   no match -- UdpSocket is a TYPE whose\n                                       methods are catalogued, not an\n                                       `attributes:` entry of module std::net\n  rust:std::fs:0-0:module              no match -- an import is not a call\n\nTHE REAL DEFECT THIS ITEM FOUND, and it is a graph-identity one, not a\ncatalogue one. Both spellings become SEPARATE EXTERNAL NODES for one library\nentity, in a single run's node set:\n\n  rust:std::net::UdpSocket:0-0:module:external_symbol   reference_syntax=module\n  rust:std.net:0-0:std.net.UdpSocket:external_symbol    reference_syntax=attribute\n  rust:std::fs:0-0:module / rust:std:0-0:std.fs         same pair\n  rust:std::io::Write:0-0:module / rust:std.io:0-0:std.io.Write   same pair\n\nThat splits any per-dependency question (who imports UdpSocket, centrality,\nsupply-chain tier, dead-code) across two nodes. It is real and worth fixing --\nbut it is NOT what withholds the verdict, and the statement/root_cause on this\nitem should be rewritten to the node-identity claim before any work starts.\nDeliberately NOT rewritten in this entry: the sibling's producer fix removes\nthe `use`-derived dotted nodes outright, so the residual population should be\nre-measured after that lands and the statement written against what is left."  # bddd
- op: update  # 3ad8
  at: '2026-08-24T11:20:09Z'  # 3ad8
  by: agent  # 3ad8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3ad8
  clock: 3  # 3ad8
  nonce: 3ad8  # 3ad8
  set:  # 3ad8
    title: "Rust names one module in two spellings when it is both imported and scope-read ('::' on imports, '.' on module_attr_ref), splitting it into two external nodes"  # 3ad8
    fields:  # 3ad8
      statement: "One library entity must have ONE node identity in a run. A module reached both by an import and by a scoped attribute read must not become two external nodes under two spellings of its path, because every per-dependency question -- who imports it, its centrality, its supply-chain tier, whether it is dead -- is then answered over a split population."  # 3ad8
      root_cause: "CORRECTED 2026-08-24 after measuring, and the original root_cause is REFUTED. The filed cause (\"the dotted one silently fails every catalogue lookup\") is false: IoBoundaryCatalog registers BOTH separators in its qualified-name index exactly as emit_module_attribute_refs' docstring claims, and the dotted form matches -- rust:std.fs:0-0:read_to_string -> fs_read, rust:std.net.UdpSocket:0-0:send -> net_send, rust:std.env:0-0:std.env.consts -> env_read. What actually put std / std.io / std.net on the uncatalogued list was INV-pusin's producer artifacts plus rust.yaml declaring zero module_completeness (WI-lutuh); neither is a normalization problem. THE SURVIVING CAUSE IS NODE IDENTITY, and it is much smaller than filed: imports edges spell a module with '::' while module_attr_ref edges spell it with '.', so a module reached BOTH ways yields two boundary nodes for one entity. Measured after INV-pusin's fix landed (PR #504), which removed the artifact half: a crate with `use std::env;` and `std::env::consts::OS` emits rust:std::env:0-0:module:module AND rust:std:0-0:std.env:attribute in the same run. A crate with only `use` statements now emits no dotted form at all, so the population is confined to modules that are both imported and scope-read."  # 3ad8
- op: discuss  # 0d29
  at: '2026-08-24T11:20:25Z'  # 0d29
  by: agent  # 0d29
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0d29
  clock: 4  # 0d29
  nonce: 0d29  # 0d29
  message: "STATEMENT AND ROOT_CAUSE REWRITTEN to the claim that survived measurement, now\nthat INV-pusin's producer fix has landed (PR #504) and the population can be\nre-measured rather than estimated. The earlier entry on this item already\nrefuted the original root cause; this one records what is left and re-scopes\nthe item to it.\n\nMEASURED ON THE MERGED TREE, three fixtures:\n\n  1. use-statements only (this item's original fixture):\n       imports          rust:std::fs:0-0:module:module\n                        rust:std::net::UdpSocket:0-0:module:module\n                        rust:std::io::Write:0-0:module:module\n       module_attr_ref  (none)\n     The dotted duplicates this item filed are GONE. They were INV-pusin's\n     artifacts -- one `use` statement emitted in both spellings -- not a\n     normalization policy.\n\n  2. a genuine scoped read with an unrelated import:\n       imports          rust:std::collections::HashMap:0-0:module:module\n       module_attr_ref  rust:std.env..., rust:std.env.consts..., rust:std...\n     Two spellings in the run but NO shared entity, so no split node.\n\n  3. THE SURVIVING CASE -- one module reached BOTH ways:\n       use std::env;  +  std::env::consts::OS\n       imports          rust:std::env:0-0:module:module\n       module_attr_ref  rust:std:0-0:std.env:attribute\n     One module, two ids, two boundary nodes after canonicalisation.\n\nSO THE ITEM IS REAL AND MUCH SMALLER THAN FILED. It is confined to modules that\nare both imported and scope-read, and it is a GRAPH-IDENTITY defect, not a\ncatalogue one -- catalogue matching works in both spellings and is not at risk.\n\nWHY THE PRIORITY SHOULD PROBABLY DROP, stated rather than acted on\nunilaterally: the user-facing harm the original title claimed (\"the catalogued\nstdlib reads as uncatalogued\") does not exist. What remains is a split\npopulation for per-dependency questions, which nothing currently measured\ndepends on. Left at P1 for the owner to re-rank rather than quietly demoted by\nthe agent who shrank it.\n\nNOT MEASURED: how often a real Rust repository both imports and scope-reads the\nsame module (the size of the surviving population); whether cpp has the same\nsplit, since it uses the same scoped_path emitter with `using` declarations."  # 0d29
- op: discuss  # 8524
  at: '2026-08-24T14:21:28Z'  # 8524
  by: agent  # 8524
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8524
  clock: 5  # 8524
  nonce: '8524'  # 8524
  message: "CORRECTION TO MY OWN CONCLUSION FROM EARLIER TODAY. The entry above says the\nsurviving defect is graph-identity only and that the dual spelling \"is NOT what\nwithholds the verdict\". That is WRONG, and a real-repo measurement taken an\nhour later shows it.\n\nOn bellman (unmodified upstream), the uncatalogued-module list the verdict is\nwithheld over contains BOTH SPELLINGS OF THE SAME MODULE, counted as two\nseparate unexamined modules:\n\n    bls12_381.Scalar   AND  bls12_381::Scalar\n    std.io             AND  std::io\n    std.cmp.Ordering   AND  std::cmp\n    std.sync           AND  std::sync::Arc\n\nThat is 4 spurious entries out of 23 reported. So the dual spelling has a\nDIRECT user-facing cost in the disclosure, not merely a split node in the\ngraph: it inflates the list a reader is asked to act on, and every entry in\nthat list is load-bearing because `qualifying_only` is `not unknown`.\n\nWHY I GOT IT WRONG, worth recording because the method was the error. I\nmeasured the dual spelling on AUTHORED FIXTURES -- a crate with a `use` and a\ncrate with one scoped read -- and concluded the populations no longer overlap.\nOn a real crate they overlap constantly, because real code both imports a\nmodule and refers to it by scoped path. LIVE.md rule 12: the population can be\nthe artifact, and a fixture is a population.\n\nTHE PRIORITY QUESTION I DEFERRED TO THE OWNER IS THEREFORE ANSWERED IN THE\nOTHER DIRECTION: I suggested this might warrant a demotion from P1. It does\nnot. It is a measurable contributor to the noise that makes verify-claims\nunreadable on Rust repos, and it sits beside INV-juvul (first-party reported as\nthird-party, 6 of 23 on the same run) in the same list.\n\nNOT MEASURED: the dual-spelling share on repos other than bellman; whether the\nright fix is normalising at emission (one spelling per language, per\nqualified_name_axis.separator_for_language) or collapsing at the gate."  # 8524
