  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-08-24 (full text on WI-lutuh): \"yes — WI-lutuh is the long pole and needs scoping now; items 1-3 are prerequisites that make its output readable but don't substitute for it.\" This item is one of those three prerequisites, and the order the owner set is INV-juvul -> INV-rilit -> WI-nakut -> WI-lutuh scoping. The ruling also settles a scope question the project had never made explicitly: verify-claims IS meant to be usable on non-python repositories, which is what promotes WI-lutuh (14 of 15 catalogued languages declare ZERO module_completeness) from a rust footnote to the long pole."  # 28c4
- op: discuss  # 54c1
  at: '2026-08-24T19:19:41Z'  # 54c1
  by: agent  # 54c1
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 54c1
  clock: 7  # 54c1
  nonce: 54c1  # 54c1
  message: "PREREQUISITE FINDING FROM INV-juvul'S MEASUREMENT, 2026-08-24 -- read this\nbefore designing the fix, because it may change what the fix is.\n\nOn bellman (2,467 edges, cached survey kept at\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/juvul_08242026/cache-bellman/), EVERY dotted rust\nentry reaching the coverage gate is a module_attr_ref edge whose \"module\" slot\nis a TYPE PATH, not a module path. They are enum-variant and\nassociated-constant reads:\n\n  rust:crate.SynthesisError:0-0:crate.SynthesisError.AssignmentMissing   enum variant\n  rust:std.cmp.Ordering:0-0:std.cmp.Ordering.Greater                     enum variant\n  rust:std.io.ErrorKind:0-0:std.io.ErrorKind.Other                       enum variant\n  rust:ff.Field:0-0:ff.Field.ONE                                         assoc const\n  rust:bls12_381.Scalar:0-0:bls12_381.Scalar.NUM_BITS                    assoc const\n  rust:bellman.VerificationError:0-0:bellman.VerificationError.InvalidProof\n\nwhile the colon-spelled entries are all `calls` edges naming real modules:\n\n  rust:std::cmp:0-0:min          rust:std::io:0-0:Error::new\n  rust:std::iter:0-0:repeat      rust:std::sync::Arc:0-0:new\n\nSO THE \"DUAL SPELLING\" IS NOT UNIFORMLY TWO SPELLINGS OF ONE MODULE. Some of\nthe listed pairs are, but at least these are a MODULE path versus a TYPE path,\nwhich is a different fact and would not be fixed by normalising the separator.\nOf the four pairs this item names:\n\n  std.io / std::io                 dotted side is std::io::Error / ErrorKind, TYPES\n  std.cmp.Ordering / std::cmp      dotted side is the Ordering ENUM\n  bls12_381.Scalar / bls12_381::Scalar   dotted side is Scalar's assoc consts\n  std.sync / std::sync::Arc        dotted side is the std::sync::atomic PATH\n\nOnly the last looks like a genuine module-vs-module spelling split.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT CHANGE: the item's node-identity concern stands, and the\n4-of-23 verdict cost stands as measured -- those rows DO reach the gate as\nseparate entries. What it changes is the candidate root cause. Before fixing\nthe separator, verify against this item's own fixture\n(~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/rust_fidelity_probe_08232026/) whether the producer\nshould be emitting a module_attr_ref for `Type::VARIANT` at all. That is the\nINV-pusin family -- its fix removed `use` paths and return-type paths from this\nedge type and this looks like the same class of producer artifact, not a\nnormalisation problem. If it is, normalising the separator would be a\nworkaround at the wrong layer.\n\nNOT MEASURED: whether the rust analyzer emits module_attr_ref for\n`Type::VARIANT` by design or by fallthrough; whether any consumer other than\nthis gate depends on those rows."  # 54c1
